  you when you're not looking. If it's just you, nobody else can surprise you, and "notification" almost always turns
  out to mean a [3mreminder[0m — due dates, nudges, "this is overdue" — which is a much smaller feature with no accounts and
  no server strictly required.

  These are expensive to swap later. Retrofitting multi-user onto a single-user model means reworking storage, every
  task record, and the whole auth story. Going the other way is wasted effort rather than rework.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m your phrasing — "tasks [3mthey[0m care about" — reads multi-user to me, so that's my guess. But I'd
  rather you confirm than have me build accounts you never wanted.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Who uses this, and do they share tasks? → Multiple people, shared tasks[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Multi-user it is. That means a server and real persistence in the foundation — shared tasks can't live in one browser.

  Next fork: [1mscale and setting[0m, because it decides how much auth machinery the foundation carries. A handful of known
  people can use something simple — a shared workspace, invite links, maybe no passwords at all — and email delivery to
  a known list is trivial. A public product needs signup, email verification, password reset, and per-user notification
  preferences before a single notification can go out. That auth decision is one of the harder things to change later,
  since it touches every request and every task record.

  I'd guess the smaller end, given this started as an 11-line HTML file — but tell me if you're aiming bigger.
